import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Types are values, runtime and typechecker share one value space","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Types are values, runtime and typechecker share one value space","type":"adr","date":"2026-08-29T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"accepted"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/archived/0032-types-as-values.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/archived/0032-types-as-values.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/archived/0032-types-as-values.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="types-are-values-runtime-and-typechecker-share-one-value-space" tabindex="-1">Types are values, runtime and typechecker share one value space <a class="header-anchor" href="#types-are-values-runtime-and-typechecker-share-one-value-space" aria-label="Permalink to “Types are values, runtime and typechecker share one value space”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The handl language is converging on a design where the type language and the expression language are the same. Types are not special syntax; they are ordinary handl values. This is the Agda-style &quot;types are values&quot; model.</p><p>Current state:</p><ul><li><code>handl-plugin-vm-type</code> already has a pure <code>Value</code> type with <code>VFun</code>, <code>VNeutral</code>, <code>VType</code>, <code>VLit</code>, etc.</li><li>The <code>.hnl</code> runtime (<code>handl-plugin-vm-values-json</code>) only has JSON values: <code>null</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>number</code>, <code>string</code>, <code>array</code>, <code>object</code>. It has no first-class function values and no propositional equality.</li><li>Because of that split, a type like <code>type.number</code> cannot be a runtime value, even though it is natural in the typechecker.</li><li>The typed examples and docs now assume a unified model, but it cannot run yet.</li></ul><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><ol><li>A <strong>type</strong> is a collection of values. It can be applied to a value to produce a <strong>membership type</strong> — a collection of proofs. If the membership type is empty, the value is not a member.</li><li><code>is</code> is <strong>membership evidence</strong>: <code>x is T</code> is the type <code>T(x)</code>. If <code>T(x)</code> is empty, <code>x</code> is not in <code>T</code>.</li><li><code>:</code> is a <strong>membership assertion</strong>: <code>name: T = e</code> means <code>let name = e in (e is T); name</code>.</li><li>Equality <code>===</code> is a <strong>type</strong>, not a boolean. <code>x === y</code> has one value <code>refl</code> when <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> are equal, and is empty otherwise.</li><li>There is <strong>one universe</strong>: <code>Type</code>. No separate <code>Prop</code> and <code>Set</code>. A proposition is a type with at most one inhabitant. A proof is a value.</li><li>The <code>type</code> module provides the base collections: <code>type.number</code>, <code>type.string</code>, <code>type.bool</code>, <code>type.null</code>, <code>type.array</code>, <code>type.object</code>, and <code>type.eq</code> (propositional equality). <code>-&gt;</code> and <code>{}</code> are built-in syntax for function and record types.</li><li>The runtime and the typechecker share the same <code>Value</code> representation.</li><li>Type-level evaluation is <strong>pure, total, and terminating</strong>. Effectful runtime is a separate mode.</li></ol><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>Even = (n: type.number) -&gt; n % 2 === 0</code> is a type family. <code>Even(4)</code> is <code>0 === 0</code>, inhabited by <code>refl</code>. <code>Even(3)</code> is <code>1 === 0</code>, which is empty.</li><li><code>Person = { name: type.string, age: type.number }</code> is a record / sigma type.</li><li><code>n: Even = 4</code> means <code>n</code> is <code>4</code> and <code>Even(4)</code> is inhabited. The proof is erased at runtime.</li><li><code>array.every(arr, T)</code> is a library function that returns a membership type (a proof or an empty type).</li><li>Function return types are part of the function signature: <code>addOne = (n: type.number): type.number -&gt; n + 1</code>.</li><li><code>f is NumberToNumber</code> means <code>NumberToNumber(f)</code> is inhabited. Function values must carry declared parameter/return type metadata, or the type must be given as a proof.</li><li><code>type.any</code> and <code>type.unknown</code> are not in the <code>type</code> module. <code>Any = (x) -&gt; x === x</code> is ordinary Handl code. <code>Unknown</code> is an internal typecheck-host concept, not a runtime predicate.</li><li><code>type.type_of</code> is reflection and out of scope for the core design. It can be added later without changing the model.</li><li><code>handl-plugin-vm-values-json</code> will be replaced or become a thin serialization view of <code>handl-plugin-vm-type::Value</code>.</li><li>The <code>mood</code> module becomes redundant. Its runtime behavior can move into the <code>type</code> module or become ordinary handl functions.</li><li>The parser/AST needs to support <code>:</code> typed bindings, <code>is</code> as membership, and <code>===</code> as a type.</li></ul><h2 id="implementation" tabindex="-1">Implementation <a class="header-anchor" href="#implementation" aria-label="Permalink to “Implementation”">​</a></h2><p>Implemented in the active workspace:</p><ul><li><code>crates/plugin-vm-type</code> now has the unified <code>Value</code>/<code>Term</code> space with <code>Collection</code>, <code>Membership</code>, <code>Is</code>, <code>Eq</code>, <code>Refl</code>, <code>TypedLet</code>, <code>Empty</code>, primitive base collections (<code>type.number</code>, <code>type.string</code>, <code>type.bool</code>, <code>type.null</code>, <code>type.array</code>, <code>type.object</code>, <code>type.eq</code>), and <code>Null</code>/<code>Array</code>/<code>Object</code> runtime values.</li><li><code>handl-lib-type</code> re-exports the primitives and provides <code>typecheck</code> and <code>typecheck_with_prelude</code>.</li><li><code>handl-platform</code> composes the VM via the new <code>value</code> and <code>vm</code> modules, re-exporting the unified value and proof system.</li><li><code>handl-target-native</code> re-exports <code>Value</code> from the platform.</li></ul>`,12)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};